    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new util classes (`Soundex`, `Sha256Hex.lowerHex`) and the `getIdentityKey`/`findPossibleDuplicateOf` reshaping are themselves well-placed and idiomatic \u2014 Soundex lives next to Sha256Hex in `util`, and the identity-key logic stays on the `Owner` model where the old one was. The problem is what the change left behind.\n\n**1. `isHouseholdInUse` is now orphaned production code.**\nThis change removed the only caller \u2014 the household-duplicate 409 block in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (diff lines 141-152). After the edit, `ClinicService.isHouseholdInUse` (interface `ClinicService.java:102`) and `ClinicServiceImpl.isHouseholdInUse` (`ClinicServiceImpl.java:477`) have no callers anywhere in the codebase (grep finds only the declaration and definition \u2014 no production or test usage). The spec states the separate household-duplicate 409 block \"no longer applies,\" so this method's entire reason for existing is gone. A maintainer finding a public service method plus its impl, `normalizeHouseholdValue`-based address logic, and `@Transactional` wiring with zero callers will be left guessing whether it's dead or load-bearing. Since duplicate detection is now the single identity key, this method and its interface declaration should be removed as part of the same change rather than left dangling.\n\n**2. The retained `isHouseholdInUse` Javadoc now describes behaviour that no longer exists.**\n`ClinicService.java:90-101` still asserts: \"The create endpoint rejects such an owner with 409 unless it declares itself a household member (`sharesHousehold`).\" That rejection path was deleted in this very diff. If the method is kept rather than removed, this contract must be corrected \u2014 as written it actively misleads about the create endpoint's behaviour.\n\n**3. Minor: stale class-level doc on `Sha256Hex`.**\nThe class Javadoc (`Sha256Hex.java:7-11`) still says it \"Computes a truncated, upper-case hexadecimal SHA-256 digest,\" but the class now also exposes the full lower-case `lowerHex`. Worth broadening the one-line summary so it covers both helpers rather than only `upperHexPrefix`.\n\nFixing #1/#2 is the substantive one \u2014 the change is additive where it introduces the new key, but it isn't coherent until it also retires the household-duplicate code it just made unreachable.",
